WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.715
Burnout and Wellbeing.

2
00:00:01.995 --> 00:00:05.878
Day 92 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Career phase.

3
00:00:07.628 --> 00:00:08.652
The key idea.

4
00:00:08.932 --> 00:00:13.241
Burnout is not a sign of weakness, and it isn't solved by working harder.

5
00:00:13.521 --> 00:00:17.831
It builds slowly, until work that used to energise you starts to drain you.

6
00:00:18.111 --> 00:00:24.831
This page covers how to spot it, how to talk about it, and how to recover, whether it's you or someone on your team.

7
00:00:26.181 --> 00:00:26.927
Warning:

8
00:00:27.207 --> 00:00:30.215
This is a communication guide, not medical advice.

9
00:00:30.495 --> 00:00:42.634
If you feel persistently hopeless, can't sleep or function, or have thoughts of harming yourself, please talk to a doctor or mental-health professional, or reach a crisis helpline in your country right away.

10
00:00:42.914 --> 00:00:49.719
Many companies also offer a free, confidential Employee Assistance Programme (EAP): ask HR.

11
00:00:49.999 --> 00:00:52.133
Reaching out early is the right move.

12
00:00:53.483 --> 00:00:54.336
Part 1.

13
00:00:54.616 --> 00:00:56.024
What burnout looks like.

14
00:00:57.274 --> 00:01:00.453
Burnout is commonly described as having three parts:

15
00:01:01.803 --> 00:01:05.557
Exhaustion: tired all the time; rest doesn't refill the tank.

16
00:01:05.837 --> 00:01:06.755
Cynicism:

17
00:01:07.035 --> 00:01:09.936
Why bother?; distance from work, team and users.

18
00:01:10.216 --> 00:01:14.419
Reduced effectiveness: simple tasks feel hard; you doubt your skills.

19
00:01:15.769 --> 00:01:17.369
Warning signs to watch for.

20
00:01:17.649 --> 00:01:18.993
Here is a quick table:

21
00:01:19.273 --> 00:01:20.937
In yourself, and In a teammate.

22
00:01:21.217 --> 00:01:22.838
Dreading Monday from Saturday:

23
00:01:23.118 --> 00:01:24.953
Camera off, quieter in meetings.

24
00:01:25.233 --> 00:01:27.174
Working longer but getting less done:

25
00:01:27.454 --> 00:01:29.438
Messages at midnight and weekends.

26
00:01:29.718 --> 00:01:31.361
Irritable over small things:

27
00:01:31.641 --> 00:01:33.923
Short, sharp replies; more conflict.

28
00:01:34.203 --> 00:01:36.315
Can't switch off; checking Slack in bed:

29
00:01:36.595 --> 00:01:39.027
Missing deadlines they used to hit easily.

30
00:01:39.307 --> 00:01:42.230
No energy for hobbies, family, exercise:

31
00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:45.134
Cancelling leave; I'll rest after this release.

32
00:01:45.414 --> 00:01:47.739
Headaches, poor sleep, getting sick often:

33
00:01:48.019 --> 00:01:50.430
Cynical jokes about the work or company.

34
00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:53.209
Common causes in tech.

35
00:01:53.489 --> 00:01:57.415
Workload: permanent crunch, too many priorities, never-ending on-call.

36
00:01:57.695 --> 00:02:01.599
Lack of control: shifting requirements, decisions made without you.

37
00:02:01.879 --> 00:02:04.609
Lack of recognition: effort goes unnoticed.

38
00:02:04.889 --> 00:02:07.961
Unfairness: some people carry the team, others don't.

39
00:02:08.241 --> 00:02:11.825
Values conflict: being asked to ship things you believe are wrong.

40
00:02:12.105 --> 00:02:15.177
Isolation: remote work without real connection.

41
00:02:16.527 --> 00:02:17.402
Part 2.

42
00:02:17.682 --> 00:02:19.005
Talking to your manager.

43
00:02:20.255 --> 00:02:22.601
The hardest step is often saying it out loud.

44
00:02:22.881 --> 00:02:25.953
You don't have to use the word burnout if it doesn't feel right.

45
00:02:26.233 --> 00:02:29.412
Describe the impact and ask for specific changes.

46
00:02:30.762 --> 00:02:31.658
Raising it.

47
00:02:31.938 --> 00:02:32.877
You could say:

48
00:02:33.157 --> 00:02:35.205
I want to be honest with you about something.

49
00:02:35.485 --> 00:02:38.343
For the last couple of months I've been running on empty.

50
00:02:38.623 --> 00:02:45.493
I'm working late most nights, and I'm still falling behind, and it's starting to affect my sleep and the quality of my work.

51
00:02:45.773 --> 00:02:48.887
I'd like to figure out together how to make this sustainable.

52
00:02:50.237 --> 00:02:52.072
Asking for specific changes.

53
00:02:52.352 --> 00:02:53.291
You could say:

54
00:02:53.571 --> 00:03:03.363
A few things would really help: taking me off the on-call rota for the next month, and dropping or delaying the reporting feature so I can focus on the migration.

55
00:03:03.643 --> 00:03:06.907
And I'd like to take a week off after the release on the 15th.

56
00:03:08.257 --> 00:03:10.347
Come with 2 to 3 concrete requests.

57
00:03:10.627 --> 00:03:14.403
It's much easier for a manager to say yes to something specific.

58
00:03:15.753 --> 00:03:16.969
If you need time off.

59
00:03:17.249 --> 00:03:18.188
You could say:

60
00:03:18.468 --> 00:03:20.537
I need to take some time off to recover.

61
00:03:20.817 --> 00:03:25.191
I've spoken to my doctor, and I'll be on medical leave for two weeks starting Monday.

62
00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:28.137
I'll hand over my current work to Ravi tomorrow.

63
00:03:29.487 --> 00:03:31.557
You don't owe anyone medical details.

64
00:03:31.837 --> 00:03:33.245
Health reasons is enough.

65
00:03:34.595 --> 00:03:36.280
If the response is dismissive.

66
00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:37.499
You could say:

67
00:03:37.779 --> 00:03:40.275
I understand there's a lot of pressure on the team.

68
00:03:40.555 --> 00:03:45.248
But if nothing changes, I won't be able to keep doing my best work, and I want to.

69
00:03:45.528 --> 00:03:47.747
Can we look at the priorities together this week?

70
00:03:49.097 --> 00:03:53.470
If it continues, talk to HR, your skip-level manager, or the EAP.

71
00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:55.115
Your health comes first.

72
00:03:56.465 --> 00:03:57.361
Part 3.

73
00:03:57.641 --> 00:03:59.519
Recovering: what actually helps.

74
00:04:00.769 --> 00:04:04.865
Real rest: leave without laptop or Slack, not working holidays.

75
00:04:05.145 --> 00:04:09.539
Sleep first: consistent sleep and wake times; no screens in the last hour.

76
00:04:09.819 --> 00:04:12.059
Move your body: a daily walk counts.

77
00:04:12.339 --> 00:04:16.371
Hard stop each day: a fixed logoff time and a shutdown ritual.

78
00:04:16.651 --> 00:04:21.110
Reduce, don't just reorganise: drop work, don't only reprioritise it.

79
00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:25.806
Reconnect: friends, family and hobbies that have nothing to do with screens.

80
00:04:26.086 --> 00:04:30.459
Small wins: finish small, visible tasks to rebuild a sense of progress.

81
00:04:31.809 --> 00:04:37.228
Professional support: a therapist or counsellor; many EAPs offer free sessions.

82
00:04:37.508 --> 00:04:41.711
See Leave and Boundaries and Work from Home for scripts to protect your time.

83
00:04:43.061 --> 00:04:43.978
Part 4.

84
00:04:44.258 --> 00:04:46.541
Prevention: daily and weekly habits.

85
00:04:47.791 --> 00:04:49.135
Here is a quick table:

86
00:04:49.415 --> 00:04:50.503
Habit, and Why.

87
00:04:50.783 --> 00:04:53.279
Say no (or not now) to new work when full:

88
00:04:53.559 --> 00:04:55.500
Workload is the biggest driver.

89
00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:58.425
Take all your annual leave, spread across the year:

90
00:04:58.705 --> 00:05:00.519
Recovery has to be regular.

91
00:05:00.799 --> 00:05:03.039
Keep one non-work commitment every week:

92
00:05:03.319 --> 00:05:04.940
It gives you a reason to stop.

93
00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:06.351
Keep a wins file:

94
00:05:06.631 --> 00:05:08.913
Counters the nothing I do matters feeling.

95
00:05:09.193 --> 00:05:11.583
Have a weekly one-on-one with honest check-ins:

96
00:05:11.863 --> 00:05:13.335
Problems get caught early.

97
00:05:13.615 --> 00:05:16.516
Rotate on-call fairly; keep it compensated:

98
00:05:16.796 --> 00:05:18.311
Prevents silent overload.

99
00:05:19.661 --> 00:05:20.621
Part 5.

100
00:05:20.901 --> 00:05:23.375
For team leads: spotting and supporting.

101
00:05:24.625 --> 00:05:25.564
Checking in.

102
00:05:25.844 --> 00:05:26.783
You could say:

103
00:05:27.063 --> 00:05:31.329
I've noticed you've been online late a lot recently, and you seem a bit stretched.

104
00:05:31.609 --> 00:05:33.167
How are you doing, honestly?

105
00:05:33.447 --> 00:05:34.492
No judgement.

106
00:05:34.772 --> 00:05:36.671
I'd rather know early so I can help.

107
00:05:38.021 --> 00:05:38.959
Then listen.

108
00:05:39.239 --> 00:05:40.839
Don't jump straight to fixes.

109
00:05:41.119 --> 00:05:41.781
Ask:

110
00:05:42.061 --> 00:05:43.469
What would help most right now?

111
00:05:44.819 --> 00:05:45.907
Taking action.

112
00:05:46.187 --> 00:05:51.840
Remove work, not just take care of yourself: reassign tasks, push deadlines, pause on-call.

113
00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:56.728
Insist on leave for people who keep postponing it, and don't contact them during it.

114
00:05:57.008 --> 00:05:59.291
Protect them from escalations for a while.

115
00:05:59.571 --> 00:06:01.341
Follow up weekly, quietly.

116
00:06:02.691 --> 00:06:03.907
Fixing the system.

117
00:06:04.187 --> 00:06:09.158
Track overtime, after-hours messages and on-call load as team health signals.

118
00:06:09.438 --> 00:06:14.302
Plan sprints at real capacity, around 70 to 80% of theoretical.

119
00:06:14.582 --> 00:06:19.318
Push back on leadership about unrealistic timelines (see Talking to the CEO).

120
00:06:19.598 --> 00:06:24.291
Model it yourself: take leave, log off on time, don't send midnight messages.

121
00:06:25.641 --> 00:06:26.559
Watch out:

122
00:06:26.839 --> 00:06:27.649
Don't say.

123
00:06:27.929 --> 00:06:30.511
Everyone's tired, just push through this release.

124
00:06:30.791 --> 00:06:32.455
You should manage your time better.

125
00:06:32.735 --> 00:06:34.612
Let's talk about it after the launch.

126
00:06:34.892 --> 00:06:36.193
Just do some yoga.

127
00:06:36.473 --> 00:06:38.777
(on its own, without changing workload).

128
00:06:40.127 --> 00:06:41.066
Part 6.

129
00:06:41.346 --> 00:06:43.266
A note for experienced engineers.

130
00:06:44.516 --> 00:06:54.201
High performers are often the most at risk: you're trusted with everything, you find it hard to say no, and part of your identity is being the person who saves the day.

131
00:06:54.481 --> 00:06:56.679
Being sustainable is a senior skill.

132
00:06:56.959 --> 00:07:03.017
A team that depends on one person working late isn't a strong team, and fixing that is part of your job.

133
00:07:04.367 --> 00:07:05.306
Part 7.

134
00:07:05.586 --> 00:07:06.973
Practice conversation:

135
00:07:07.253 --> 00:07:09.215
Telling your manager you're overloaded.

136
00:07:10.465 --> 00:07:12.492
Here is how the conversation goes.

137
00:07:12.772 --> 00:07:15.012
Can I be honest about something?

138
00:07:15.292 --> 00:07:19.303
I've been working late every night for a month, and I'm exhausted.

139
00:07:19.583 --> 00:07:21.055
Thank you for telling me.

140
00:07:21.335 --> 00:07:23.255
I hadn't realised it was that bad.

141
00:07:23.535 --> 00:07:25.689
I'm worried it's affecting my work.

142
00:07:25.969 --> 00:07:29.447
I made two mistakes last week that I normally wouldn't.

143
00:07:29.727 --> 00:07:31.305
What would help most right now?

144
00:07:32.655 --> 00:07:34.255
The conversation continues.

145
00:07:34.535 --> 00:07:39.677
Moving the reporting feature to next sprint, and taking me off on-call for two weeks.

146
00:07:39.957 --> 00:07:41.194
I can do both.

147
00:07:41.474 --> 00:07:43.906
And please take a few days off after the release.

148
00:07:44.186 --> 00:07:45.125
Thank you.

149
00:07:45.405 --> 00:07:46.706
That really helps.

150
00:07:48.056 --> 00:07:49.592
That's the end of this lesson.

151
00:07:49.872 --> 00:07:54.523
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

152
00:07:54.803 --> 00:08:00.925
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

